/* ============================================================================
   limit-of-ln1plusx-over-x.epher -- ln(1 + x)/x tends to 1 at x = 0

   The standard limit ln(1 + x)/x -> 1 as x -> 0 (Stewart, "Calculus",
   8th ed., sec. 3.8 exercises): it is the derivative of ln x at x = 1.
   Probed at x = 1e-2, 1e-4 and 1e-8; the last sample trails 1 by about
   1.1e-8 because adding x to 1 rounds at the 16th decimal digit, which
   then divides back out only approximately.

   The builtin derivative of ln at 1 is the same limit, so it is printed
   as a cross-check.

   Accuracy: samples print at about the 12th digit; the deficit at
   x = 1e-8 is an artifact of rounding 1 + x, not of the limit.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/calculus/limits/limit-of-ln1plusx-over-x.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\calculus\limits\limit-of-ln1plusx-over-x.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/calculus/limits/limit-of-ln1plusx-over-x.epher
   The knob to edit is `x` below.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ---------------------------------------------------------------
const x = 1e-8

// ---- ln(1 + x)/x at the three sample sizes ----------------------------------
ln(1 + x)/x
for t in {1e-2, 1e-4, x} do ln(1 + t)/t
abs(ln(1 + x)/x - 1) < 1e-6

// ---- cross-check: derivative of ln at 1, on a fresh variable t -------------
// (x is bound by the knob above; t is free).
derivative(ln(t), 1)

/* ---- expected output ---------------------------------------------------------
= 0.00000001
= 0.999999988923
= {0.995033085317, 0.999950003333, 0.999999988923}
= true
= 1
*/
